<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frei Caneca</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1779">1779</a>–<a href="/years/1825">1825</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Joaquim do Amor Divino Caneca</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frei Caneca, born Joaquim do Amor Divino Rabelo, was a Carmelite friar and journalist from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recife">Recife</a>. He took part in the Pernambucan revolt of 1817 and was imprisoned until 1821. In 1824 his newspaper, the Typhis Pernambucano, made him the leading voice of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-equator">Confederation of the Equator</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-i-of-brazil">Emperor Pedro I</a>. After its defeat he was condemned to death and shot in Recife in January 1825.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frei-caneca.jpg" alt="Frei Caneca" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frei_Caneca.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
